# Survival Analysis: Time-to-Event in the Employee Lifecycle

## Welcome {.unnumbered}

"Will this employee leave?" is the question most attrition work starts
from. It's the wrong question to *start* with, because it throws away
the most useful part of the data: **when**. Someone who leaves after two
months and someone who leaves after eleven years are both just "a leaver"
to a yes/no model — but they're very different stories, and a model
that only sees yes/no can't tell them apart.

This chapter introduces **survival analysis**: the family of methods
built specifically to model *time until an event*, handling gracefully
the one thing every attrition dataset has and most other methods choke
on — people who **haven't left yet**.

## What you'll be able to do by the end {.unnumbered}

1.  Explain **censoring** and why ignoring it biases your conclusions
2.  Read a **Kaplan-Meier curve** — the standard first look at
    time-to-event data
3.  Fit a **Bayesian parametric survival model** in `brms` with
    covariates
4.  Interpret a survival model's coefficients in plain business language
5.  Recognise where the same technique needs adapting for a *positive*
    event (time-to-promotion) that not everyone will ever experience

